0	About

This package contains a version of the Charniak parser that has been modified
to output a packed forest for each individual sentence, originally developed
in my work on forest reranking:

Liang Huang (2008). 
Forest Reranking: Discriminative Parsing with Non-Local Features.
In Proceedings of ACL 2008, Columbus, OH.

This tool is freely available for research use, as long as you cite both the 
above paper and the following Charniak and Johnson paper in your published work
that use it:

Eugene Charniak and Mark Johnson (2005). 
Coarse-to-Fine n-best Parsing and Maxent Discriminative Reranking.
In Proceedings of ACL 2005, Ann Arbor, MI.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------

I	Usage

(To compile the parser, simply type "make" in the first-stage/PARSE dir.)

Then go to first-stage/, there are testcases "test.*".

The new command line option for dumping forest is "-f<FILE>]", which
dumpes the forests to <FILE>, with a blank line separating forests.

For example, in the top directory of the parser, run

 PARSE/parseIt -K -l399 -ftest.out DATA/EN/ test.txt \
	| grep . | perl -ple 's/^\(S1/\(TOP/g'

Notes:

0. -K: keep tokenization (for Treebank parsing). -l399: max. length is 399.

1. There should be no space between -f and <FILE>. 
	Running the above command should produce a file "test.out" that is 
	identical to the reference output "test.forest" in this directory.

2. as specified in Charniak parser, the input file must contain lines like:

		<s> ... </s>

   if you would like to supply a plain input without <s> and </s>, 
	simply replace testfile by:
	
	    <(cat testfile | perl -nle 'print ("<s> $_ <\/s>")') \

   But for large-scale experiments, it is recommended to use the <s> format
	since it can annotate the sentence by a tag like <s wsj_0000.0>	which can
	be used for identifying the forest (see below section II).

3. as specified in Charniak parser, the output is separated by a blank line 
	between sentences, and each parse is embraced by (S1 ... ). So I use grep
	and perl to filter/edit the output.

4. The original Charniak parser is able to use multithreading, but for the 
	forest mode, it currently runs only in singlethread mode. So even if you
	specify "-T2", the parser will treat it as "-T1" internally (and prints 
	a warning).


----------------------------------------------------------------------------

II	Output Format

The forest file has a blank line between the forests, each of which have 
the following format:

<sentence_tag>		<sentence>
<#_of_nodes>
<nid>	<label> [<left>-<right>]	<#_of_hyperedges>
		<list_of_tail_nodes>  |||  0=<log_prob>
		...
...

For example,

wsj_0000.0		I saw her duck . 
134
1       NN [0-1]        0
2       NP [0-1]        1
        1  ||| 0=-4.20609

...

5       NP* [0-1]       2
        2  ||| 0=-8.80136
        4  ||| 0=4.20521

...

132     SINV [0-5]      2
        88 106 107  ||| 0=-12.5642
        88 6 130 131  ||| 0=-10.9256
133     S1 [0-5]        2
        85  ||| 0=-0.0205583
        132  ||| 0=-5.29062
134     S1* [0-5]       1
        133  ||| 0=-1.17614

Notes:

1. Multiple spaces denote a TAB.

2. <sentence_tag> is an optional tag for the input sentence, e.g.:
	
	<s wsj_0000.0> I saw her duck . </s>

	it will not show up in the forest file if it is missing from the input, 
	i.e., if you supply <s> I ... </s>. (see above, section I)

3. The <nid> (node_id) starts from 1, but is not guaranteed in general
	to increase monotonically. The only guarantee is the topological ordering:
	all nodes refered (in the tail list of a hyperedge) are already defined.

4. Each node has a nonterminal label, like NP, followed by an optional *.
 	Nodes marked with a * are called "spurious nodes" in the Charniak parser.
	They *always* have unary hyperedges from non-spurious counterparts, and
	they *always* appear in the right-hand-side when they are non-heads, i.e.,
	there are rules like the following in the grammar:
		
		NP* -> NP		(cost 1)
		VP -> VBD NP*	(cost 2)

	I strongly recommend to remove them by a script (See Section A.1), 
	resulting in an equivalent forest without these nodes, 
	possibly at the cost of more hyperedges. Basically, the idea is to bypass
	spurious nodes by connecting directly to non-spurious ones, for example:
	
		VP -> VBD NP	(cost 1+2=3)

5. The spans of nodes use C-style indices, where [0-3] means:
	word_0, word_1, and word_2.

6. A node is followed by zero or more hyperedges, each of which will have a 
	list of children node Ids. In the above example, node 2 (NP [0-1]) has one 
	hyperedge, which is from node 1 (NN [0-1]), while node 132 (SINV [0-5])
	has two hyperedges, which having four childrens.

7. A node has no hyperedges *if and only if* it is a part-of-speech tag.

8. In a hyperedge, after the list of tails, one or two spaces and 
	a separator ("|||") and some white spaces, there comes the 
	weight(s) of this hyperedge in the form of
		
		"0=<log_prob>"

	here 0 is a "feature_id". In the case of Charniak parser, there is only 
	one feature, the log_probability, but you can imagine in the reranking
	phase there will be something like:
		
		"0=-14.234 1000=2 5000 6000=-1 ..."

	here the format is "<feature_id>[=<feature_value>]" where the default 
	feature value is 1 (as most features are binary).	 

----------------------------------------------------------------------------

III	Some Notes on the Implementation

This modification is relatively easy because the Charniak parser already 
implemented k-best parsing Algorithm 3 of Huang and Chiang (2005), which
requires storing the forest (alternative hyperedges leading to a node).

Details of this part (esp. re: Bst, Val) will come soon.

under PARSE/ dir:
	
	parseIt.C: main loop, entrance point
		
		void printSentenceAndTag(ofstream & fout, SentRep * srp)

			print to fout the sentence tag (specified in <s ...> from input), 
			followed by TAB, and the sentence itself.

		void printSkippedForest(ofstream & fout, SentRep * srp, MeChart * chart)

			in case of parse failure, still output a flat FRAG forest.			
			POS tags are supplied by chart.

		mainLoop() calls printForest(fout, bst, srp) in Bst.C.

			bst probably means "best", which seem to correspond to 
			a node in the forest (along with its 1-best derivation).
			here in mainLoop, bst points to the TOP node.

	Bst.C:
		
		void printForest(ofstream & fout, Bst & bst, SentRep & sr)

			main function for the major work of forest dumping.
	
			1. prepare all nodes (which is reachable from TOP (bst)) 
				and hyperedges, by calling a recursive function
					bst.prepareNode(visited, sr, start, cells, startid, 0)
				which in turn prepares all the descendants.

			2. print all nodes and hyperedges.

		void Bst::prepareNode(set<Bst*> & visited, SentRep& sr, int & pos, \
			Cellstype & cells, int & count, int dep)
		
			recursively prepare hyperedges of each node.

	Bst.h:

		  BSType BS; // incoming hyperedges (Backward-Star)


----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Appendix

A. Post-Processing

	These Python scripts are drawn from my forest reranker package, 
	which is to be released separately. They are currently in ../forest-stage/.

	General Test:

		cat <forest_file> | ../forest-stage/forest.py -k <K>

	for dumping <K>-best parses of each forest, which is useful to test if the forests
	we got are correct (by comparing the k-best list with those from Charniak parser, 
	modulo floating point bounding errors, which usually happens on the 4th digit).

	A.1 Spurious Node Removal
		
		cat <forest_file> | ../forest-stage/remove_sp.py 	

		Note: S1 are also changed into TOP after this step.

	A.2 Forest Pruning
	
		cat <forest_file> | ../forest-stage/prune.py -p <log_prob>

		Here <log_prob> is the (log) prob. beam you allow. 
		For a hyperedge, if the best derivation that traverses through it
		is e^{<log_prob>} worse than the global 1-best derivation, then it
		will be pruned.

		In my ACL 2008 paper I used a threshold of 10.

	Example:
	
		cat test.forest | ../forest-stage/remove_sp.py \
			| ../forest-stage/prune.py -p 5 > test.p5
		diff -bd test.p5 test.forest.removed.pruned-p5
		
		** should be identical **
		
